Disco Code
==========

Disco Code is an open source, local-inference coding agent distributed under the
MIT License. It is a derivative work combining upstream MIT-licensed
projects. This file records which parts of the tree derive from which upstream,
so that license inheritance is auditable.

All upstreams are MIT licensed, and MIT permits derivative works and
relicensing under the same terms provided the original copyright and permission
notices are retained. Disco Code therefore remains MIT and reproduces those
notices in LICENSE.


Upstream 1: Claw Code
---------------------
Source:    https://github.com/ultraworkers/claw-code
Version:   v0.1.3
License:   MIT
Copyright: (c) 2026 UltraWorkers and Claw Code contributors

Derived components:

    crates/api/              LLM transport layer and provider abstraction
    crates/commands/         Slash-command surface
    crates/plugins/          Plugin discovery, hooks, lifecycle
    crates/runtime/          Session, permissions, policy engine, MCP,
                             sandbox, prompt construction, compaction
    crates/telemetry/        Local telemetry primitives
    crates/tools/            Built-in agent tool implementations
    crates/rusty-claude-cli/ Terminal client
    crates/claw-rag-service/ Local retrieval/embedding service

The prompt-construction pipeline in crates/runtime/src/prompt.rs is inherited
from Claw Code and is central to Disco Code's answer quality. It is retained and
extended rather than replaced.


Upstream 2: opencode
--------------------
Source:    https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode
Version:   v1.18.10
License:   MIT
Copyright: (c) 2025 opencode

Derived components:

    desktop-ui/src/themes/   Theme definitions (37 files, verbatim)

Disco Code adopts opencode's design system and its SolidJS/Vite frontend stack.
It deliberately does not adopt opencode's backend protocol.

That boundary was chosen by measurement rather than preference. opencode's
frontend reaches its backend through 56 HTTP endpoints whose payload shapes are
generated from Effect schemas and whose handlers are the opencode TypeScript
core: session model, event bus, permissions, PTY, and tool output store.
Reproducing that surface in Rust would mean reimplementing opencode's core to
byte-compatible JSON and then tracking it on every upstream release, for a
product whose core already exists in Rust.

The packages divide cleanly along that line, which is what made the split
practical:

    packages/ui           193 source files, zero imports of opencode's
                          sdk, protocol, core, or Effect -- a presentation
                          layer with no protocol coupling. Adopted.
    packages/session-ui    93 files, 19 protocol-coupled.
    packages/app          420 files, 123 protocol-coupled -- this is the
                          HTTP client application itself. Not adopted.

The theme files are the portion taken verbatim. They are pure data and carry no
runtime dependency. The interface components are written against the Rust core's
own IPC surface rather than copied, because the copied versions would have
carried the protocol assumptions the split exists to avoid.

opencode's own desktop application is Electron. Disco Code uses Tauri instead,
so the interface is the platform webview rather than a bundled browser. This is
a departure from opencode, not an inheritance from it.


Upstream 3: oh-my-codex
-----------------------
Source:    https://github.com/Yeachan-Heo/oh-my-codex
License:   MIT
Copyright: (c) 2025 Yeachan Heo

Derived component:

    crates/runtime/src/enhance.rs   Staged prompt-enhancement harness

The project brief attributed prompt-enhancement logic to Claw Code. It is not
there: Claw Code builds a conventional single system prompt, and its Python
tree is port-status metadata rather than behaviour. The staged approach —
interviewing for ambiguity, planning before editing, hardening irreversible
work, then verifying against stated criteria — originates in oh-my-codex, which
implements it as a workflow layer around a different agent. No oh-my-codex code
is copied; the design is reimplemented in Rust, and the attribution is recorded
here because the design is the borrowed part.

Two deliberate departures: stage selection is triaged per request rather than
always running the full pipeline, so simple edits stay one turn; and triage is
deterministic heuristics rather than a model call, because judgement is the
first capability to degrade at local model sizes.


Deliberate exclusions
---------------------
Neither upstream's cloud-model integrations are inherited. Disco Code performs
inference exclusively against a local Ollama daemon. Hosted-model providers,
their credential/OAuth flows, and hosted billing or account infrastructure are
removed rather than disabled, and an egress policy prevents cloud AI endpoints
from being reached at runtime.

Specifically, the following Claw Code components are not carried forward:

    crates/api/src/providers/anthropic.rs   Anthropic Messages API client
    crates/mock-anthropic-service/          Mock Anthropic service used to
                                            exercise the above
    crates/api/tests/client_integration.rs  Anthropic wire-protocol test suite
    rusty-claude-cli mock parity harness    Parity tests against the mock

along with, inside retained files, the xAI/OpenAI/DashScope endpoint
configurations and their credential handling, the hosted model registry and its
aliases, the subscription-provider OAuth token flow, and the `/login`,
`/logout`, and `/api-key` command surfaces. Remote MCP server authentication is
unrelated to the above and is retained.

The Claw Code retrieval service previously embedded repository contents through
a hosted endpoint by default; it now embeds against the local daemon, so
indexing a repository does not transmit source code.

This is a local-inference product, not an offline one. Network access for web
search, web fetch, code search, and Model Context Protocol servers is expected
and supported.
